AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP turns db_read and net_recv into the label
untrusted_input. So these rows did not merely mislabel a call -- they MINTED
TAINT at call sites that observe nothing, and every flow downstream of an
invented source is a false positive carrying a real-looking value chain. That
is the mechanism behind measurement 0006's headline being an upper bound on
useful precision rather than a measure of it.

F1 (WI-rigut) 31 haskell rows declared Data.IORef / Data.STRef /
   Control.Concurrent.MVar / Control.Concurrent.STM as db_read/db_write.
   Process memory: no descriptor, no syscall, nothing outside the process can
   observe them, and newIORef is a CONSTRUCTOR that reads nothing. Haskell rows
   no real database anywhere, so this removes a claim it never had rows to make.

F2 (WI-dosov) socket / bind / listen declared net_recv in ten languages.
   socket() creates an endpoint, bind() names it, listen() marks it passive.
   None receives a byte; recv/recvfrom/recvmsg do and are kept.

F3 (WI-hikut) JPA EntityManager.create*, JDBC Connection.createStatement and
   all nine Ecto.Query combinators declared db_read while returning a builder
   that issues no statement.

BOTH OPEN QUESTIONS IN THE DECLARED-DEBT REGISTER ARE ANSWERED, NOT RE-RULED,
AND THE REGISTER IS NOW EMPTY. haskell's STM rows carried
`boundary_ruling: unruled` asking whether "the db_* classification of the whole
Data.IORef / STRef / MVar / STM family is right at all -- a vocabulary decision
beyond this row". erlang's gen_udp.open asked "is a socket-acquisition call a
net_* crossing at all?" No, to both. The erlang row had deferred itself on
INVERTED reasoning -- "removing them is a recall change and this is a
precision-band cycle" -- but a source row minting taint from an operation that
transfers nothing is a PRECISION defect, and removing it is what a
precision-band cycle is for.

THE RULE THAT DECIDES F3 IS "IS THE READ STILL REPRESENTED AFTER THE REMOVAL",
not "does this call issue SQL". JPA rows TypedQuery.getResultList, Ecto rows
Ecto.Repo.all, so their builders relocate the read rather than delete it.
DJANGO IS DELIBERATELY KEPT and fails that test: a QuerySet is lazy, so
filter() issues nothing, but execution is an implicit __iter__ with NO call site
to catalogue -- removing it would delete the read, not move it. JAVASCRIPT IS
EXCLUDED FROM F2 for the same reason: its entire net_recv surface is callback
registration (http.createServer, Deno.listen, WebSocket.onmessage) with no
transfer row anywhere. Both are pinned as tests that cost something.
`accept` is KEPT and ruled, not overlooked: it returns the peer address, which
is chosen by whoever connected, so it is a real source.

